Mount Elbrus is the highest peak in Europe and was my third climb of the [Seven Summits](sevensummits/). Everything about this trip to Russia was unforgettable. It felt surreal to experience the food, drinks, people, sights and sounds of Moscow and the Caucasus first hand. In more stable times I’d love to travel a lot more of the country, ideally on the Trans-Siberian Express, but we always felt safe and welcome in the areas we did visit. Even on that brutal climb!

Our preparation went well, including six days of acclimatising in the mountains, but summit night is still incredibly difficult. The elevation gain is double that of [Mont Blanc](mont_blanc), and 1.5 times that of [Kilimanjaro](Kilimanjaro), while also being steeper and more technical with lots of exposure and some fixed lines. It was a proud moment when Adam Creagh & I persevered to reach the summit on July 29th 2024. 

This is the story of that climb, including all the lessons we learned along the way. 

#### Background
Elbrus is a dormant volcano in the Caucasus Mountains of Russia, about 10km from the Georgian border. It’s the tenth most prominent peak in the world, with its iconic twin summits dwarfing everything else in the area. From the top you can see both the Black Sea & Caspian Sea on a clear day. It’s the highest point in Russia and, as it lies west of the Eurasian border in the Ural Mountains, is the highest point in Europe.

The name derives from the Turkish *Alburoz* meaning white mountain, and the Persian *Alborz* meaning High Sentinel (also the name of a mountain range in Iran). In Greek mythology it’s where Prometheus was chained by Zeus and tortured as punishment for stealing fire from the Gods. In local legend it’s said that the once singular peak was split in two as Noah’s Ark passed over it.

The mountain is covered in heavy year-round snow, which filled any volcanic craters long ago. Strong winds bring the cold, difficult conditions the mountain is known for. As such, winter ascents are rare. In 1829, a Russian army scientific group was first to reach the lower East Summit at 5,621m. It wasn’t until 1874 that an English expedition found a safe route across the deep saddle to reach the West Summit at 5,642m. 

Elbrus has since been a training ground where some of the world’s best mountaineers prepared for the Himalayas, including Anatoli Boukreev. It played a part in history as a battleground in World War 2, hosted international races that pitted the Soviets against Americans, and seen records like horseback summits and the highest ‘driven’ Land Rover, which summited in 1997 after 43 days of being winched up! 

By 2020, the low prices and dramatic scenery had made the Caucasus very popular with tourists. In summer it’s known for world class hiking, mountaineering and the famous thermal baths that give the closest city its name; Mineralnye Vody meaning *Mineral Waters*. In winter it’s known for skiing and snowboarding, being close to Sochi which hosted the 2014 winter Olympics. Tourism has turned much more domestic now, but is no less busy.

#### Preparation & Planning
Two routes are most popular because they minimise crevasse and avalanche risk. The North Route is more remote and adventurous, but traverses the lower East peak where some guides have turned groups if not wanting to cross the saddle. They claim this counts as the summit at only 21m shorter, but rather than find ourselves arguing this we chose to avoid that risk. We opted for the South Route which follows Europe’s highest cable car from the town of Cheget to Garabashi Station at 3,800m, then up into the saddle and directly to the summit.

Ascents are typically done between June & September. We opted for July for more consistent weather. We booked an all-inclusive guiding package with Elbrus Tours that would pick up and drop off in Mineralnye Vody, simplifying logistics.  Ekaterina in their office was incredibly helpful in the lead up, and the trip was made very simple while there. The eight-day trip included all transport, accommodation, food, drinks and mountain guiding for a cost $1,050 each.

Getting a Russian visa was a very easy process, for Irish at least. They use an online e-visa platform and I had mine approved and back one hour after applying. The cost was $52. 

Booking flights was less easy, which means it’s finally time to address the elephant in the room. Since the Russian invasion of Ukraine in 2022 several sanctions have affected what tourism to Russia looks like. The big one for us is that you can’t use Visa, Mastercard or send money to a Russian account from any western bank. I had been offered a local Russian MIR card by someone who foresaw the headaches we’d have but we decided to make it work without. Here’s what this looked like:

Our flights to and from Moscow were via Istanbul with Turkish Airlines. Most of the world still flies there and it was easy to book, we just lost the cheap Ryanair options. Internally we had a return flight to Mineralnye Vody with Aeroflot, this was booked through a broker company in Dubai. We emailed a hotel in Moscow and arranged to pay with cash on arrival, and sent our deposits to Elbrus Tours via their Western Union contact in Bulgaria with rest in cash on arrival. 

My favourite mountaineering insurance is through the Austrian Alpine Club, but all western companies refuse to cover travel to Russia right now. We got an Elbrus specific travel, rescue and medical insurance through Save Pro Life who are based in Moscow. They obviously had no issue with the mountain and deal regularly with foreign climbers. We had a lot of trouble paying but thankfully Ekaterina paid on our behalf and we reimbursed in cash when we arrived. The cost was $100 each.

